        <div id="mah-vs-wh" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. mAh vs Wh: How Many Phone Charges You Really Get
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power banks are sold by milliamp-hours because the numbers look big, but mAh is a poor way to compare them. A milliamp-hour figure only means something when you also know the voltage it was measured at, and power bank makers quote the internal cell voltage, usually 3.6 to 3.85 volts. Your phone charges at 5 volts or more over USB, so some energy is lost converting up, and more is lost as heat in the cable and inside the phone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The number that actually tells you how much energy is in the box is watt-hours. To convert, multiply mAh by the cell voltage and divide by 1,000. A 10,000mAh bank at 3.7 volts holds about 37Wh. A 20,000mAh bank holds about 74Wh. Power stations and the larger DC batteries skip the confusion entirely and are sold by Wh, which is why a 288Wh unit can be compared directly with a 245Wh one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, assume you will get roughly 60 to 70 percent of a power bank's rated energy into your device. That loss is normal, not a defect, and it is why a 10,000mAh bank never gives you two full charges of a 5,000mAh phone. A typical current phone battery holds around 13 to 19Wh, so we use 15Wh as a middle figure in the table below. LiFePO4 power stations and DC batteries convert more efficiently, so we use about 85 percent for those.
          </p>

