        <div id="what-it-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What a Power Bank Can and Can't Run in an Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank is a USB battery. It can run or charge anything that charges from USB, and with the right adapter cable it can run a handful of low-power 12V devices. It cannot run anything with a motor, a heating element or a compressor, and it cannot run anything that needs a wall outlet unless it has a built-in inverter, which most banks do not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What works well</h3>

          <p>
            Phones, tablets, earbuds, smartwatches, e-readers and laptops are the core job. USB-powered LED lanterns and string lights are extremely efficient: a 3W LED lantern runs for well over a day on a 20,000mAh bank. USB fans draw 2 to 5W and make a summer outage far more bearable for sleeping. Rechargeable headlamps and flashlights can be topped up as you go. All of this costs very little energy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Wi-Fi router question</h3>

          <p>
            Keeping the internet up during an outage is possible if your provider's equipment in the neighborhood still has power, which is often the case for fiber and sometimes for cable. Most home routers run on 12V and draw roughly 6 to 12W. A USB-C PD trigger cable that requests 12V from the bank can run a router directly. A 24,000mAh bank delivering around 70Wh at 12V runs a 10W router for about six to seven hours. If you also need to power a separate modem or fiber ONT, budget for both devices, or step up to the 288Wh SOLIX C300 DC, which runs both for well over a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CPAP machines</h3>

          <p>
            A CPAP is the device people most often hope a power bank can handle, and in most cases it cannot. Most CPAPs need either AC power or a specific DC input voltage and adapter, and with the humidifier on they can draw 60W or more through the night. A machine running at 30 to 40W with the humidifier off needs around 300 to 400Wh for an eight-hour night including losses. That is power station territory. Our guide on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-cpap/">whether a portable power station can run a CPAP</a> walks through the math and the DC adapter options for the common machines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refrigerators, sump pumps and heaters</h3>

          <p>
            No power bank will run a refrigerator. A fridge compressor draws 100 to 200W while running and several times that for a moment at startup, and it cycles all day. That takes a large power station, typically 1,000Wh and up, or a generator. See <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">can a portable power station run a refrigerator</a> for real runtimes. The same goes for sump pumps, space heaters, microwaves and well pumps. If those are on your list, a power bank is the wrong tool and you should be reading our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> guide instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="usb-c-pd" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. USB-C PD Wattage: What the Numbers Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            USB-C Power Delivery, or PD, is the standard that lets a charger and a device agree on a voltage and current. A power bank's headline wattage, 20W, 30W, 65W, 140W, is the most it can deliver through a single port when the device asks for it. Older USB-A ports top out around 12 to 18W for most devices, which is why a modern bank's USB-C port is the one to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            For phones, 20 to 30W is all you need. Most iPhones charge at roughly 20 to 27W peak, and even phones advertised at 45W only hit that briefly. A 30W bank like the Anker Nano charges any current phone at full speed. Tablets benefit from 30 to 45W. Laptops are where wattage starts to matter: an ultrabook may be happy at 45 to 65W, while a 16-inch performance laptop wants 100 to 140W. A bank with too little wattage still charges a laptop, but slowly, and if the laptop is working hard it may only slow the drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to total versus per-port output. A bank advertised at 140W might deliver 140W from one USB-C port but split that when two devices are plugged in. For outage use, where you are often charging several phones at once, that is fine, but do not expect full laptop speed while three phones share the same bank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cables matter too. A standard USB-C cable is rated for 60W. To get 100W or more, you need a cable rated for 5A, often labeled 100W or 240W. Keep at least one high-wattage cable in your outage kit, plus one short cable for each phone type in the house. A great bank with no matching cable is useless in a dark house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for pass-through charging if you want the bank to sit on a charger and run a device at the same time, which is useful for a router setup. Many banks support it, but it generates heat and can shorten battery life if used constantly, so treat it as an outage feature rather than an everyday habit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-planning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Capacity Your Household Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with how long outages typically last where you live. Most utility outages in the US are measured in hours, but storm outages regularly run two to four days, and major hurricanes or ice storms can leave neighborhoods dark for a week or more. Plan for the outage you have actually had, then add a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            A careful adult can stretch one phone charge to a full day by using low power mode, dimming the screen and closing apps. Budget one full charge per person per day as a floor, and two if anyone works from a phone or relies on it for medical apps. For a family of four planning for three days, that is 12 to 24 phone charges, or roughly 180 to 360Wh delivered.
          </p>

          <p>
            That math quickly shows why one pocket bank is not enough. A practical setup for a family of four is one 10,000mAh personal bank per person plus one large shared bank such as the Anker 737, which together deliver around 150Wh, or about 10 phone charges. That covers a typical one-to-two-day outage comfortably. For three days or more, or if you want the router running, add a 288Wh battery like the SOLIX C300 DC, which roughly doubles your reserve on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laptops change the picture. A single laptop charge uses about as much energy as four phone charges. If someone in the house needs to work through an outage, budget a full 20,000mAh-class bank just for the laptop each day, or plan around a power station. And if anyone depends on powered medical equipment, plan that first and separately, because it should not compete with phones for the same battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keep-charged" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Keeping Power Banks Charged for Emergencies
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common outage story we hear is not about a bad power bank. It is about a good one found dead in a drawer. Lithium batteries slowly lose charge while sitting unused, typically a few percent a month for a quality bank, and more in heat. A bank charged in spring can be well below full by hurricane season, and some cheap banks drain much faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is a routine. Top up every bank in the house every three months, and tie it to something you already do. The daylight saving changes in March and November are an easy hook, since many people already test smoke alarms then, and the start of hurricane or winter storm season is another. Put a recurring reminder on your phone and check the display or LEDs on each bank when it goes off.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage conditions matter as much as charge level. Heat is the main enemy of lithium batteries. Do not store power banks in a car in summer, on a sunny windowsill or in a hot attic. A closet or drawer at room temperature is ideal. Cold reduces available capacity temporarily but does far less permanent harm. For an emergency bank that you want ready to grab, storing it near full is the right tradeoff, even though batteries that sit at 100 percent age slightly faster than those kept at half charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            LiFePO4 batteries, used in the SOLIX C300 DC and River 3, are more forgiving. They are rated for far more cycles than standard lithium-ion and tend to hold charge better in storage, which is one reason they make sense as the bulk energy reserve in an outage kit. Our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion comparison</a> covers the chemistry differences in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            When an outage warning comes, charge everything immediately: every bank, every phone, every laptop, the weather radio and any rechargeable flashlights. A few hours of warning before a forecast storm is the cheapest energy you will ever store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="airline-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Airline Limits and the 100Wh Rule
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your outage charger will also travel with you, capacity in watt-hours decides whether it can fly. Under FAA rules, spare lithium batteries, which includes power banks, must go in carry-on bags, never in checked luggage. Banks rated at 100Wh or less are allowed in carry-on without airline approval for personal use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Banks between 100Wh and 160Wh require airline approval, and passengers are generally limited to two of them. Anything over 160Wh, which includes the SOLIX C300 DC and every portable power station, is not allowed on passenger aircraft at all. That is why so many large banks top out just under 100Wh: 26,800mAh at 3.7V works out to about 99Wh, and the Anker 737 sits at about 88.8Wh.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for the Wh rating printed on the bank's case. Security screeners and airline staff check it, and a bank with no visible rating can be refused. Airlines outside the US and individual carriers may add their own rules, including restrictions on using or charging power banks in flight, so check before you go.
          </p>

          <p>
            For outage planning, the practical takeaway is simple. A bank of 26,800mAh or less is the largest that can serve double duty for travel, while anything above that should be thought of as a home battery that stays home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Outage Kit Checklist
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power bank is one part of a working outage kit, and it is only as useful as the gear around it. Keep everything in one bin or bag in a known spot, so whoever is home can find it in the dark without asking.
          </p>

          <p>
            For charging, include one 10,000mAh bank per person, one large shared bank of 20,000mAh or more, a short USB-C cable and a short Lightning cable if anyone still uses one, one 100W-rated USB-C cable for laptops, and a USB-C PD to 12V trigger cable matched to your router's barrel plug if you want internet during an outage. A small wall charger that can refill a bank quickly is worth adding, so you can bank energy whenever power briefly returns.
          </p>

          <p>
            For information and light, include a NOAA weather radio with a hand crank, such as the Midland ER310, spare AA batteries for it, one LED lantern per room you plan to use, and a headlamp per adult. Headlamps are the most underrated outage item: they keep both hands free and use very little energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the house, add a printed list of emergency contacts and your utility's outage reporting number, since you may not want to spend phone battery searching for them. Include a refrigerator thermometer, because food safety in an outage depends on knowing whether the fridge stayed at or below 40 degrees F. Our guide to <a href="/what-temperature-should-a-refrigerator-be/">what temperature a refrigerator should be</a> has the details.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, write down when you last charged everything, and keep that note in the kit. It turns the quarterly top-up from a vague intention into a quick check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Step Up to a Power Station or Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power banks are the right tool for keeping personal electronics alive. You need something bigger once your list includes anything with a wall plug that draws real power, or once your outages regularly stretch beyond two days. The dividing line is usually around 300Wh and AC outlets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step up to a portable power station if you need to run a CPAP through the night, keep a router and modem on for days, power a small fan or lamp from an outlet, or run a fridge for part of the day. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> guide ranks the options from compact to whole-home, and our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">power station sizing guide</a> helps you add up your devices and pick a capacity that actually covers them.
          </p>

          <p>
            A power station also changes how you recharge. Most accept solar panels, so during a long, sunny outage you can refill the station every day and keep the whole system going. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a portable power station with solar</a> covers panel sizing and real-world recharge times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step up to a generator, or a large battery system, if you need to keep a refrigerator and freezer cold for days, run a sump pump, a well pump or a furnace blower, or keep a window air conditioner running in summer. Those loads are measured in thousands of watt-hours per day, which is beyond any battery you can carry. Read <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power station vs generator</a> for a side-by-side comparison, then our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> guide for specific picks.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">USB devices: power bank. Wall plugs: power station. Motors and heat: generator.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If everything you need runs from a USB port, a few good power banks cover you. Once you need outlets for a CPAP, a fan or a router brick, move to a power station. Once a compressor, pump or heater is on the list, plan for a generator.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by mAh alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 20,000mAh bank does not give you four charges of a 5,000mAh phone. Conversion losses mean you get about 60 to 70 percent of rated energy, so expect roughly three charges. Compare watt-hours, and plan with delivered energy rather than the big number printed on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the bank go flat in a drawer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium banks slowly self-discharge, and heat speeds it up. A bank charged last spring may be half empty when a storm hits. Top up every bank quarterly, tie it to the daylight saving changes or storm season, and charge everything the moment an outage is forecast.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a power bank to run appliances</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No USB power bank runs a refrigerator, sump pump, space heater or microwave, and most cannot run a CPAP. Those loads need a power station or a generator. Use banks for phones, laptops, lights, fans and the router, and size a bigger system for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the cables</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fully charged bank is useless if nobody can find a cable that fits. Keep a dedicated set of short cables in the outage kit, including a 100W-rated USB-C cable for laptops and a 12V trigger cable for the router. Built-in cable banks avoid the problem entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on hand-crank charging for phones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hand cranks and small solar panels on emergency radios produce very little energy. A minute of cranking buys minutes of radio, not a meaningful phone charge. Treat crank radios as information and light, and store your phone energy in proper power banks charged ahead of time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable charger for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, a 20,000 to 24,000mAh USB-C bank like the Anker 737 is the best single pick. It delivers three to four phone charges, charges laptops at full speed, and stays under the airline limit. Add a small bank per person, and step up to a 288Wh battery or power station for outages beyond two days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many times will a 10,000mAh power bank charge my phone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually 1.5 to 2 full charges for a typical current phone. A 10,000mAh bank holds about 37Wh, but conversion losses mean around 24Wh actually reaches the phone. Most phones hold 13 to 19Wh. Wireless charging, cold temperatures and an older phone with a larger battery all reduce the number of charges you get.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power bank run a Wi-Fi router during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with the right cable. Most routers run on 12V and draw 6 to 12W, so a USB-C PD trigger cable set to 12V can power one from a bank. A 24,000mAh bank runs a 10W router about six to seven hours. Your internet provider's equipment must still have power for the connection to work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power bank run a CPAP machine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Most CPAPs need AC power or a specific DC adapter and draw 30 to 60W or more, which needs around 300 to 500Wh for a full night. That is power station territory. A compact unit like the EcoFlow River 3 can run many machines with the humidifier off. Check your model's DC options first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a power bank hold its charge in storage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality lithium power bank typically loses a few percent per month in storage at room temperature, more in heat. That means a bank can be well short of full after six months. Top it up every three months, store it indoors away from heat, and charge everything the moment an outage is forecast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the largest power bank allowed on a plane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under FAA rules, power banks of 100Wh or less are allowed in carry-on bags without airline approval. Banks between 100 and 160Wh need airline approval, with a limit of two. Anything over 160Wh is prohibited on passenger aircraft. Power banks are never allowed in checked luggage, and the Wh rating should be printed on the case.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power bank or a power station better for outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you need to run. Power banks are cheaper, lighter and ideal for phones, laptops, lights and a router. A power station adds AC outlets and much more capacity, so it can run a CPAP, fans and small appliances. Many households keep both: banks for personal devices, and a station for everything with a plug.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

